  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nSurface high under northwest flow aloft will continue to push\nacross the state through tonight. Expect dry conditions as a\nresult with lows in the 50s tonight. Mainly clear skies are also\nexpected as the afternoon CU field in the north and east\ndissipates. HRRR Smoke showing some near surface smoke possible\nin the southwest tonight, with smoke aloft also moving in. It\nmay take some mixing to get smoke to the surface, thus limited\nsurface smoke through the evening hours tonight. Did adjust sky\ncover to account for some smoke aloft through the night.\nSaturday, a broad surface low lifts a weak warm front. This will\nbring warmer temperatures in the 80s north to the 90s south.\nGenerally dry conditions will allow for some low afternoon\nhumidity values in the teens and 20s southwest, with above 30\npercent elsewhere. With the surface low forecast to track across\nsouthern ND, generally light winds are expected except late\nafternoon and evening when northerly winds on the back side of\nthis low could increase somewhat. This may bring a brief period\nof near critical fire weather conditions in the far southwest.\nConfidence isn't high enough for any fire products at this time.\nMeanwhile, along this warm front in the southeast will be some\nincreased instability and shear, although the surface will be\ngenerally capped. There still could be some elevated\nthunderstorms late day through the evening. SPC has now clipped\nparts of southeastern ND with a Marginal Risk to account for \nthis potential. Hail and strong winds will be the main threats \nwith any isolated storm that develops. Some weak showers could \nalso develop along a stalled front in the north during the \nafternoon Saturday, although the threat for severe weather in \nthese areas is low. Surface high then moves a weak cold front \nthrough Saturday night. This should diminish the thunderstorm \nthreat in the southeast, although a lingering low level jet will\nprovide for some slight PoPs. Otherwise look for lows in the \n50s along with a steady easterly wind. Smoke aloft could linger \nSaturday through Saturday night. Better mixing in the southwest \ncould bring this smoke to the surface Saturday. Cooler \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to upper 80s, and mainly dry \nconditions will then be found for Sunday, along with a steady \neasterly wind. These cooler temperatures should bring improved \nRH values, limiting the fire weather concerns. A stalled surface\nfront could eventually bring some showers and thunderstorms, \nmainly later Sunday through Sunday night. SPC has clipped the \nfar south with a Marginal Risk. Most of the instability holds \noff until the evening and Sunday night hours, perhaps indicating\nsome stronger storms at night are possible. Low temperatures \nlook to maintain in the 50s Sunday night. \n\nClusters are in fair agreement for zonal flow next week as the\nwestern ridge flattens. Some clusters push a trough extending\nfrom a Hudson Bay low into the area as well. Overall this\npattern looks to support near to below average temperatures\nalong with near daily chances for isolated to scattered showers\nand thunderstorms. Depending on strength of passing waves in\nthis zonal flow will greatly depend on rain and rain amount\nchances. CSU-MLP also showing at least some isolated severe\nchances in the southern half of ND through next week although\nthese will also greatly depend on these mentioned waves. NBM\nshowing low to medium chances for a wetting rain each day, and\nare somewhat increased later next week especially in the west.\nCooler temperatures could provide for some lower RH values, \nwhich may also help to limit the fire weather concerns through\nnext week.",
